"""Pathology and alternate names (tier 0.5l).

Edit freely — see data/__init__.py for how files are loaded."""


_PATHOLOGY = {
    'coagulative necrosis': 'Coagulative Necrosis — MCC of necrosis; ischemia/infarction (except brain); cell architecture preserved (ghost outlines) but cells dead; acidophilic cytoplasm, pyknotic/karyorrhectic nuclei; caused by ↓blood flow → enzymatic digestion limited; affects: heart (MI), kidney, spleen infarcts; tissue firm; eventually replaced by fibrous scar',
    'liquefactive necrosis': 'Liquefactive Necrosis — enzymatic digestion dominates → tissue liquefies (no structural preservation); causes: (1) brain infarction (neurons rich in lipids + proteolytic enzymes → liquid center → cystic cavity); (2) bacterial/fungal abscesses (neutrophil proteases → pus = dead neutrophils + debris + fluid); creamy yellow pus; complete liquefaction',
    'caseous necrosis': "Caseous Necrosis — 'cheese-like' gross appearance; amorphous granular eosinophilic material with no structural detail; specific for granulomatous infections: TB (most important), fungi (Histoplasma, Coccidioides); surrounded by epithelioid macrophages + Langhans giant cells (rim of palisading macrophages around caseous center); TB Ghon complex = lung focus + calcified hilar lymph node",
    'fibrinoid necrosis': 'Fibrinoid Necrosis — vascular wall necrosis; bright pink (eosinophilic) fibrin-like material in vessel walls; immune complex deposition + complement activation; causes: malignant hypertension (arterioles), vasculitis (PAN, ANCA-vasculitis), serum sickness; glomerulonephritis (crescent formation); rheumatoid nodules; indicates severe vascular injury',
    'fat necrosis': 'Fat Necrosis — saponification; lipase cleaves fat → free fatty acids + glycerol → combine with Ca²⁺ → chalky white calcium soap deposits; (1) Pancreatic fat necrosis: acute pancreatitis, serum lipase ↑, peritoneal fat necrosis + chalk-like deposits; (2) Breast fat necrosis: trauma, prior surgery → firm mass ± calcifications on mammogram (can mimic carcinoma)',
    'lewy bodies': 'Lewy Bodies — intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions composed of alpha-synuclein + ubiquitin; found in: substantia nigra (Parkinson disease), cortex (Lewy body dementia — visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, cognitive fluctuations, REM sleep behavior disorder); halo appearance on H&E; alpha-synuclein immunostain; Parkinson: Braak staging (starts olfactory bulb/dorsal vagal nucleus → ascends)',
    'negri bodies': 'Negri Bodies — intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions in neurons; pathognomonic for rabies encephalitis; found in: hippocampal pyramidal cells (Sommer sector) + Purkinje cells of cerebellum; composed of viral nucleocapsids; clinical: encephalitic (furious) vs paralytic (dumb) rabies; Babes nodules = perivascular lymphocytic cuffing; dx: direct fluorescent antibody test on brain biopsy',
    'mallory bodies': 'Mallory-Denk Bodies (Mallory Hyaline) — intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions of misfolded intermediate filaments (cytokeratin 8/18 + p62 + ubiquitin); found in: alcoholic liver disease (classic), NASH/NAFLD, Wilson disease, primary biliary cholangitis, focal nodular hyperplasia, hepatocellular carcinoma; hepatocyte ballooning + Mallory bodies = key feature of alcoholic/NASH hepatitis',
    'councilman bodies': "Councilman Bodies (Acidophil Bodies) — eosinophilic apoptotic hepatocyte remnants in hepatic sinusoids; seen in: viral hepatitis (yellow fever — classic 'Councilman body'), all viral hepatitides; hepatocyte undergoes apoptosis → shrunken eosinophilic body; in yellow fever: midzonal necrosis + Councilman bodies; also called acidophil bodies or hepatocyte apoptotic bodies",
    'aschoff bodies': 'Aschoff Bodies — pathognomonic granulomas of acute rheumatic fever; fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by: Aschoff giant cells (Langhans-type multinucleated giant cells) + Anitschkow cells (caterpillar/owl-eye chromatin in macrophages) + lymphocytes + plasma cells; found in: myocardium (most important — rheumatic carditis), pericardium, endocardium; cause: group A Streptococcus molecular mimicry (M protein vs cardiac antigens)',
    'psammoma bodies': "Psammoma Bodies — concentrically laminated calcified spherules (calcium carbonate); 'PSaMMoma' mnemonic: Papillary thyroid carcinoma + Serous papillary cystadenocarcinoma of ovary + Meningioma (meningothelial type) + Mesothelioma; also: prolactinoma, papillary RCC; represent dystrophic calcification in necrotic tumor cells or psammomatous meningiomas; seen on H&E and cytology",
    'russell bodies': 'Russell Bodies — distended ER filled with immunoglobulin in plasma cells; eosinophilic globular inclusions; normal variant of plasma cells with high Ig production; when numerous: Mott cells; seen in: chronic inflammation (plasma cell-rich infiltrates), multiple myeloma, inflammatory conditions; adjacent nuclear equivalent: Dutcher bodies (intranuclear Ig pseudoinclusions)',
    'heinz bodies': "Heinz Bodies — denatured hemoglobin precipitates attached to RBC membrane; NOT visible on standard Wright-Giemsa stain; require supravital stain (crystal violet, new methylene blue); seen in: G6PD deficiency after oxidative stress, alpha-thalassemia (HbH disease), unstable hemoglobin variants; 'bite cells' seen on Wright stain as macrophages bite out Heinz bodies from RBC",
    'howell-jolly bodies': 'Howell-Jolly Bodies — nuclear remnants (DNA fragments) in RBCs; normally removed by spleen; seen in: functional/anatomical asplenia (sickle cell disease — autosplenectomy, post-splenectomy, celiac disease), megaloblastic anemia, myelodysplastic syndrome; appear as small dark purple spheres on peripheral smear; indicates absent/reduced splenic function',
    'auer rods': "Auer Rods — abnormal azurophilic needle-like inclusions in cytoplasm of myeloid blasts; composed of fused lysosomes (myeloperoxidase, azurophilic granules); pathognomonic for AML (not ALL); MOST prominent in AML-M3 (APML/acute promyelocytic leukemia — multiple Auer rods = 'faggot cells'); DIC risk in M3 from releasing granule contents; dx: cytochemistry MPO+, FISH for t(15;17), PML-RARA",
    'reed-sternberg cells': "Reed-Sternberg Cells — binucleate or multinucleate giant cells with prominent eosinophilic 'owl-eye' nucleoli; pathognomonic for classical Hodgkin lymphoma; CD15+ and CD30+ (but CD20-, CD45-); derived from germinal center B cells; surrounding reactive inflammatory infiltrate (lymphocytes, eosinophils, plasma cells); variants: lacunar cells (nodular sclerosis), lymphocytic/histiocytic (L&H/'popcorn') cells in NLPHL",
    'owl eye inclusions': "Owl-Eye Inclusions — large intranuclear inclusion surrounded by clear halo; classic for CMV (cytomegalovirus); found in: CMV pneumonitis, CMV colitis, CMV retinitis (AIDS), CMV hepatitis; cells are enlarged (cytomegaly); also: intracytoplasmic inclusions; Reed-Sternberg cells also described as 'owl-eye' (different context — Hodgkin lymphoma)",
    'dystrophic calcification': 'Dystrophic Calcification — calcium deposition in dead/dying/abnormal tissue; serum Ca²⁺ NORMAL; occurs in: atherosclerotic plaques, heart valves (calcific AS), old TB granulomas (eggshell calcification), fat necrosis (breast, pancreas), cysticercosis (ring calcification), dermatomyositis (subcutaneous), Psammoma bodies in tumors, areas of liquefactive necrosis',
    'metastatic calcification': "Metastatic Calcification — calcium deposition in NORMAL tissue; caused by ↑serum Ca²⁺ or ↑phosphate; occurs in: interstitium of kidney, lungs, gastric mucosa, blood vessels, cornea (band keratopathy); causes: hyperparathyroidism, hypervitaminosis D, sarcoidosis, milk-alkali syndrome, prolonged immobilization, malignancy; 'metastatic' refers to widespread distribution, NOT cancer metastasis",
    'congo red staining': 'Congo Red Staining — stains amyloid; apple-green birefringence under polarized light (pathognomonic); positive for all types of amyloid; amyloid SAA (secondary, chronic inflammation), AL (plasma cell dyscrasia/myeloma), ATTR (transthyretin — hereditary or senile), Aβ (Alzheimer), IAPP (T2DM islets), β2-microglobulin (dialysis); serum/urine protein electrophoresis + biopsy (abdominal fat pad, rectal, kidney) for diagnosis',
    'amyloid': 'Amyloid — misfolded protein fibrils in β-pleated sheet configuration; Congo red stain → apple-green birefringence (polarized light); types: AL amyloid (light chains — plasma cell dyscrasia, myeloma; kidney + heart + liver + tongue macroglossia + peripheral neuropathy); AA amyloid (SAA protein — chronic inflammation: RA, IBD, FMF; kidney predominant); ATTR (transthyretin — senile cardiac or hereditary neuropathy); Aβ (Alzheimer — neuritic plaques)',
    'keratin pearls': 'Keratin Pearls — concentrically arranged squamous cells with central keratin; pathognomonic for well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma; represent abortive attempt at keratinization; found in: SCC of lung (central, smoking-related), SCC of head/neck/skin/cervix/esophagus; H&E: pink concentric whorls; intercellular bridges + individual cell keratinization also hallmarks of SCC',
    'signet ring cells': "Signet Ring Cells — malignant cells with eccentric nucleus pushed to periphery by intracytoplasmic mucin vacuole; pathognomonic for mucinous/signet ring adenocarcinoma; types: (1) diffuse gastric carcinoma (linitis plastica — 'leather bottle stomach') — CDH1 mutation, hereditary diffuse gastric cancer, poor prognosis; (2) colorectal mucinous adenocarcinoma; (3) lobular breast carcinoma (also intracytoplasmic mucin, E-cadherin loss); Krukenberg tumor = gastric SRC mets to ovary",
    'giant cells': 'Giant Cell Types in Pathology — Langhans giant cell: nuclei arranged in horseshoe/peripheral pattern → TB and other granulomatous infections; Foreign body giant cell: nuclei randomly scattered → foreign material; Touton giant cell: ring of nuclei with foamy cytoplasm peripherally → xanthomas, juvenile xanthogranuloma; Reed-Sternberg cell: owl-eye nucleoli → Hodgkin lymphoma; Warthin-Finkeldey: virocytes → measles',
    'granuloma': "Granuloma — organized collection of activated macrophages (epithelioid histiocytes) ± Langhans giant cells ± surrounding lymphocytes; mechanism: macrophage cannot digest → chronic activation; caseating granuloma (with central caseous necrosis): TB, fungi (Histoplasma, Coccidioides); non-caseating granuloma: sarcoidosis, Crohn's disease, berylliosis, foreign body reaction, cat scratch disease",

    'ghon complex': "Ghon Complex — primary tuberculosis; consists of: Ghon focus (subpleural parenchymal lesion, usually the lower part of an upper lobe or the upper part of a lower lobe) + hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy; together = Ranke complex; Ghon focus: area of caseating granuloma; calcifies in immunocompetent → 'popcorn' calcification on CXR; hallmark of primary TB exposure; most primary TB is contained but may progress in immunocompromised; Ghon complex visible on CXR as Ghon focus + hilar calcified nodes",
    'kimmelstiel-wilson lesion': "Kimmelstiel-Wilson Lesion — nodular glomerulosclerosis; pathognomonic of diabetic nephropathy; hyaline nodules in mesangium of glomerulus (PAS-positive, eosinophilic); oval/round nodules replace mesangial matrix; also: diffuse mesangial expansion (more common but less specific); 'capsular drop' and 'fibrin cap' lesions also seen; efferent > afferent arteriolar hyalinosis (specific for diabetes); EM: basement membrane thickening + mesangial expansion; progression: microalbuminuria → macroalbuminuria → ESRD",
    'wire loop lesion': "Wire Loop Lesion — histologic finding in lupus nephritis (LN) class IV/III; subendothelial immune complex deposits thicken glomerular capillary walls to resemble 'wire loops'; PAS-stain shows thick capillary walls; immunofluorescence: 'full house' positivity (IgG, IgA, IgM, C3, C1q — classic for SLE); EM: subendothelial electron-dense deposits; Class IV (diffuse proliferative LN): most severe, most common, wire loops prominent; tx: induction with mycophenolate or cyclophosphamide + steroids → maintenance mycophenolate",
    'call-exner bodies': "Call-Exner Bodies — small fluid-filled spaces surrounded by granulosa cells; 'rosette-like' or 'gland-like' arrangement in granulosa cell tumors of ovary; eosinophilic secretion in center; associated with granulosa cell tumor (most common ovarian sex cord-stromal tumor); secretes estrogen → endometrial hyperplasia/carcinoma; presents as postmenopausal bleeding or precocious puberty; inhibin + (tumor marker); coffee bean nuclei (longitudinal groove)",
    'pannus': "Pannus — vascularized granulation tissue arising from synovium in rheumatoid arthritis; composed of: activated fibroblast-like synoviocytes (FLS) + macrophages + T cells + B cells + plasma cells; invades and destroys cartilage and bone at joint margin; produces proteases (MMPs) + RANKL (→ osteoclast activation → bone erosion); imaging: juxta-articular erosions (marginal erosions) on X-ray; MRI: pannus = enhancing synovial tissue; target of biologic therapy: anti-TNF (adalimumab), anti-IL-6 (tocilizumab), anti-CD20 (rituximab)",
    'foam cells': "Foam Cells — lipid-laden macrophages; key component of atherosclerotic plaque; formation: monocytes infiltrate intima → differentiate into macrophages → take up oxidized LDL via scavenger receptors (SR-A, CD36 — not downregulated unlike LDL receptor) → become foam cells → release cytokines/proteases → form fatty streak → fibrous plaque; also in: xanthomas (Achilles tendon in familial hypercholesterolemia), xanthelasma (periorbital), Niemann-Pick (sphingomyelin-laden macrophages); ruptured plaque → thrombus → MI/stroke",
    'mallory-weiss tear': "Mallory-Weiss Tear — longitudinal mucosal laceration at gastroesophageal junction caused by sudden ↑intragastric pressure; typically from forceful vomiting/retching; associated with alcohol use, eating disorders, hyperemesis gravidarum; presents as hematemesis after episode of retching; usually bleeds from left gastric artery branches; usually self-limiting (80-90% stop spontaneously); endoscopy: linear tear at GEJ; tx: supportive → PPI; endoscopic therapy (epinephrine injection, thermal coagulation, clips) if active bleeding; rarely surgical",
    'crescent formation': "Crescent Formation — crescentic glomerulonephritis; proliferation of parietal epithelial cells + infiltrating macrophages/monocytes in Bowman's space; compresses and obliterates glomerular capillaries; caused by rupture of glomerular capillary wall (fibrin leaks into space → triggers proliferation); PAS stain: cellular crescents (early, active) → fibrocellular → fibrous (sclerotic); causes of rapidly progressive GN (RPGN): Type I (anti-GBM — linear IF, Goodpasture), Type II (immune complex — granular IF, lupus/MPGN), Type III (pauci-immune — few/no deposits, ANCA — granulomatosis with polyangiitis, MPA)",
    'rokitansky-aschoff sinuses': "Rokitansky-Aschoff Sinuses — mucosal herniations through muscular layer of gallbladder wall; visible on histology and ultrasound; associated with gallbladder adenomyomatosis (hyperplastic cholecystopathy); on US: 'comet-tail' reverberation artifacts posterior to echogenic foci; not malignant; cholesterolosis: cholesterol deposits in macrophages in gallbladder wall (strawberry gallbladder appearance); adenomyomatosis: fundal thickening with R-A sinuses, benign but can mimic carcinoma on imaging",
    'apoptosis': "Apoptosis — programmed, energy-dependent cell death WITHOUT inflammation; cell shrinkage, chromatin condensation (pyknosis), nuclear fragmentation (karyorrhexis), membrane blebbing, apoptotic bodies eaten by macrophages; intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway: ↑BAX/BAK, ↓BCL-2 → cytochrome c release → caspase-9; extrinsic pathway: Fas ligand or TNF → death receptors → caspase-8; both → executioner caspases 3, 6, 7; examples: embryogenesis, thymic selection, Councilman bodies (viral hepatitis), cytotoxic T-cell killing (perforin/granzyme); BCL-2 overexpression → follicular lymphoma t(14;18)",
    'necrosis': "Necrosis — unregulated cell death after injury (ischemia, toxins, infection) with membrane rupture and INFLAMMATION (unlike apoptosis); patterns: coagulative (most organs — ischemia), liquefactive (brain, abscess), caseous (TB, fungi), fat (pancreatitis, breast trauma), fibrinoid (vasculitis, malignant hypertension), gangrenous (dry = coagulative limb ischemia; wet = added infection); signs of irreversible injury: membrane damage, mitochondrial calcium deposits, nuclear pyknosis → karyorrhexis → karyolysis",
    'cellular adaptations': "Cellular Adaptations — reversible responses to stress: hypertrophy (↑cell size — cardiac muscle in hypertension, skeletal muscle with exercise), hyperplasia (↑cell number — endometrial hyperplasia from unopposed estrogen, BPH; may progress to cancer in some tissues), atrophy (↓size or number — disuse, denervation, ischemia, aging; ubiquitin-proteasome system + autophagy), metaplasia (one adult cell type replaced by another — Barrett esophagus, squamous metaplasia in smokers' bronchi, vitamin A deficiency; reversible but can progress to dysplasia), dysplasia (disordered growth — pleomorphism, ↑nucleus:cytoplasm ratio, loss of polarity; precancerous, e.g. cervical CIN; carcinoma in situ = full-thickness dysplasia without invasion)",
    'acute inflammation': "Acute Inflammation — fast response (minutes–days) led by neutrophils (first 24–48 h), then macrophages; cardinal signs: rubor, calor, tumor, dolor, loss of function; vascular phase: arteriolar dilation (histamine, PGI2, NO) + ↑permeability (histamine, bradykinin, leukotrienes C4/D4/E4) → exudate; leukocyte extravasation: rolling (selectins) → tight adhesion (integrins, LFA-1/ICAM-1 — defective in leukocyte adhesion deficiency) → transmigration (PECAM-1) → chemotaxis (C5a, LTB4, IL-8, bacterial peptides); phagocytosis (opsonins IgG, C3b) and killing by the NADPH-oxidase respiratory burst (defective in chronic granulomatous disease); IL-1, IL-6 and TNF cause fever and acute-phase proteins (CRP, fibrinogen, ferritin, hepcidin)",
    'chronic inflammation': "Chronic Inflammation — prolonged (weeks–years) inflammation with lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages, with ongoing tissue destruction and repair (fibrosis, new vessels); causes: persistent infection (TB, viruses), autoimmune disease, foreign material; granulomatous inflammation is a special form (epithelioid macrophages ± giant cells, driven by Th1 and IFN-γ); consequences: AA amyloidosis, anemia of chronic disease (hepcidin), ↑cancer risk (ulcerative colitis → colon cancer, Barrett esophagus, H. pylori → gastric cancer and MALT lymphoma)",
    'wound healing': "Wound Healing — phases: hemostasis (minutes), inflammation (days 1–3: neutrophils then macrophages), proliferation (days 3–21: granulation tissue — fibroblasts, new capillaries via VEGF, type III collagen; re-epithelialization; myofibroblast contraction), remodeling (weeks–1 year: type III replaced by type I collagen via zinc-dependent collagenases; scar regains ~70–80% of original strength); primary intention (clean apposed edges) vs secondary intention (open wound, more granulation and contraction); impaired by infection (most common), diabetes, ischemia, steroids, malnutrition, vitamin C, zinc or copper deficiency; hypertrophic scar (stays within the wound, type III collagen, regresses) vs keloid (extends beyond the wound, types I and III, earlobes, more common in people of African descent, recurs after excision)",
    'oncogenes': "Oncogenes — gain-of-function mutations of proto-oncogenes (one mutant allele is enough) that drive growth: growth factors (PDGF — astrocytoma), receptors (HER2/ERBB2 — breast, gastric; RET — MEN2, papillary thyroid; KIT — GIST; EGFR — lung adenocarcinoma), signal transducers (KRAS — pancreas, colon, lung; BRAF V600E — melanoma, hairy cell leukemia, papillary thyroid; JAK2 — myeloproliferative neoplasms), transcription factors (MYC — Burkitt t(8;14); MYCN — neuroblastoma), cell-cycle regulators (cyclin D1 — mantle cell lymphoma t(11;14)), anti-apoptotic genes (BCL-2 — follicular lymphoma t(14;18)), fusion kinases (BCR-ABL — CML t(9;22), treated with imatinib; ALK — lung adenocarcinoma, anaplastic large cell lymphoma)",
    'tumor suppressor genes': "Tumor Suppressor Genes — loss-of-function; BOTH alleles must be inactivated (Knudson two-hit hypothesis): RB1 (retinoblastoma, osteosarcoma — controls G1→S via E2F), TP53 ('guardian of the genome' — G1 arrest via p21 and apoptosis; Li-Fraumeni syndrome; mutated in ~50% of cancers), APC (FAP, colorectal cancer — Wnt/β-catenin), BRCA1/2 (DNA repair — breast, ovarian, prostate, pancreatic), VHL (clear cell renal carcinoma, hemangioblastoma), NF1 (Ras GTPase-activating protein), NF2 (merlin), WT1 (Wilms tumor), PTEN (Cowden syndrome — breast, endometrial, thyroid), CDKN2A/p16 (melanoma, pancreatic), SMAD4/DPC4 (pancreatic), MEN1 (menin)",
    'embolism': "Embolism — a detached mass carried in the blood to a distant site: thromboembolism (most common — DVT → PE; left heart or atrial fibrillation → systemic emboli), fat embolism (long-bone or pelvic fractures, after 24–72 h — hypoxemia, confusion, petechial rash), amniotic fluid embolism (labor or delivery — sudden hypoxia, shock, DIC; fetal squames in maternal lung vessels), air embolism (central line insertion/removal, diving — decompression sickness; place the patient left side down and head down), cholesterol embolism (after arterial catheterization in atherosclerosis — livedo reticularis, AKI, eosinophilia, 'blue toes'), septic emboli (endocarditis), tumor emboli",
}

